- Context construction: prompt = retrieved cross-file chunks (top-k snippets, from other files, each with a file path comment) + current file context up to the cursor (typically truncated to about 256–1024 or 2048 tokens). Official default: cross_file with max prompt length 1024 (for some models); in the paper, they vary cross-file context length from 0 to 4096. For next-line: target line completion; metrics: exact match (EM) of completed line, edit similarity (via python-diff / Levenshtein-based, official code's "calculate_edit_similarity"), and BLEU. pass@k is not in the official RepoBench next-line protocol (it's for InfiBench-style execution tasks); RepoEval has no execution harness → recommend EM + edit similarity as primary; pass@k only optional / out-of-scope.
- Decoding: official uses greedy? The RepoEval evaluation script uses top_p sampling with temperature and n samples (they report EM / edit similarity of best-of-n? Actually they compute metrics per sample; generate once, or sample with temperature 0.2?). The official evaluate script uses default top_p=0.2? What I recall from the RepoEval evaluation script: generation with default top-p sampling. Mark as uncertain; propose greedy decoding as primary control + sampled n completions for variance.
